this morning as we continue highlighting  the best new books every week david  sedaris  the best of me is sedaris's handpicked  collection of his favorites  it is hilarious touching and crass some  of the odd enduring qualities that have  made him so famous over the years  before the pandemic he was the only  author we knew of who could fill concert  venues just so  people could listen to him read out loud  during the pandemic david sedaris is  still finding things to laugh about  starting with zoom thank you for not  doing a zoom interview  well i i did one  but my policy was to do none but  and it wasn't that satisfying is it for  anyone  i think some lazy people prefer it like  they say oh you don't have to wear pants  and you have to leave the house and i  think how lazy can you get  david sedaris's writing has changed over  the years  did i tell you about the burglar who got  stuck in the chimney  but he has never lost his brutal sense  of humor  so who was this burglar i'd ask did they  identify his body  he was a gypsy a drifter and on two  occasions  an arab this is the first time sedaris  has performed for someone  in person since the pandemic began he  met us in new york's central park  it's also the first time he signed his  much sought-after personalized copies of  books in months  his newest collection is called the best  of me  one thing i realized is with this book  you can  add to the me and so you can have the  best of mexico  the best of mediocrity the best of  meningitis  sedaris's jokes have never been for  everyone but sometimes  based on the crowds you feel like it's  close  millions have found something special in  the way he's been writing about his life  and family for the last 30 years  he's produced 11 best-selling books  including barrel fever  naked and let's explore diabetes with  owls  most of those books have been followed  by sold out concert tours across the  country  at last night's signing i was approached  by a teenage girl who asked me to  autograph  what was without question the ugliest  t-shirt i had ever seen in my life  and even the occasional commencement  address choose  one thing to be terribly terribly  offended by this as opposed  to the dozens or possibly hundreds that  many of you are currently juggling  do you prefer performing in touring or  writing i always think of performing and  touring as my reward for writing  so it's my reward for being alone do you  miss touring  i miss it so much i miss even  delayed flights and crummy hotels  i i wonder if it's possible to die from  lack of attention  i i say to my boyfriend just applaud for  me i'm going to walk into the room and i  want you to applaud  i just miss it so much i miss  attention sedaris's early work focused  mostly on his childhood  he was born in upstate new york and  raised in north carolina  his mother who battled alcoholism died  when sedaris was in his early 30s  seven years ago his sister tiffany died  by suicide  a story included in the best of me which  he says is his favorite  because he got a chance to share the  toll of her mental illness  assadera's tradition of rarely holding  back feelings about family  he calls his father just shy of 100 and  recently recovered from a near-death  experience  cold and mean my father's 97  this is what people say oh like that  oh you're so lucky and so my father  can't be visited now in his assistant  living center right  so i write him letters and then  i talked to him a while ago and he said  i got those last two letters  you sure didn't have anything to say i  thought  who else is writing you nobody else is  writing you letters  that's how i know he was better you know  that he could just be  he could be mean i thought hey dad's  back  does your family like you well  i bought them a beach house so they  better like me i mean i think sometimes  people got the idea like oh i'm glad i'm  not in your family  but when i write something about my  family it's always something they know  is funny  you don't do social media i have a  facebook page but i've never looked at  it  and i think i have a twitter account  i've never looked at that either  and i think it makes me a happier person  this collection of essays represents  just a small body of his work  and will likely again hit the best  seller list but these days there's  something else  sedera says he wants to be remembered  for when he's not in new york  he and his partner hugh hamrick are at  their home in the english countryside  where sedaris picked up some work as a  volunteer  i was out riding my bike one day and i  saw a guy picking up rubbish and i said  uh  what are you doing and he said i'm  picking this up i said are you paid and  he said no it just drives me crazy  and i thought well i can do that so i  started doing it that very day  the town even recently named a dump  truck in his honor  i usually spend when i'm in england  between four and eight hours a day  picking up trash on the side of the road  and that's what you want  david or the david sedaris legacy to be  i'd be happy if that was it it gives me  an incredible  sense of accomplishment i think it's  more worthwhile than anything else that  i do  pretty cool huh four to eight hours a  day picking up trash on the side of the  road  david sedaris hates this word awesome  but he is awesome i i  just want to do this for him yes there  you go david we've been reading him  the whole family since the 90s and and  still do  and the writing has gotten more serious  um and more emotional so it's been  interesting to watch that journey for  him  yeah i hate litter bugs i'm all the way  i'm  coming over helping you pandemonium and  david working together  i'm out there that there it is and there  we go

